.TH CHTTPSVR_SUBROUTER 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_subrouter \- create a sub-router with a path prefix
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_router *chttpsvr_subrouter(chttpsvr srv, const char *prefix);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new router under the given path prefix and attaches it to
the server.  The prefix must start with \&'/'.  A route registered on
the sub-router is matched against the part of the path after the
prefix, and the sub-router owns every path under the prefix; see
NOTES.

The prefix must not contain two consecutive slashes, as in "//api" or
"/a//b".  Such a prefix can never match a valid HTTP path and would
silently create a router that nothing can reach, so
chttpsvr_subrouter rejects it and returns NULL.

A trailing slash on the prefix is removed automatically, so
"/api/v1/" and "/api/v1" are equivalent and give the same routing
behaviour.

The server owns the returned pointer, and the caller must not free
it.  Every chttpsvr_router_on, chttpsvr_router_on_stream and
chttpsvr_router_use call on that router first checks that this server
has not been destroyed, and the check also covers a concurrent destroy
from another thread, so it is safe to keep the pointer across a
restart with chttpsvr_stop and chttpsvr_start.  A registration call
that races a concurrent chttpsvr_destroy of srv returns
ccol_invalid_args and touches no freed memory.

This function is thread-safe and can be called both before and after
the server starts.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A mounted prefix owns every path under it.  A path is under a prefix
when its decoded form (the one that chttpsvr_req_path reports) equals
the prefix or continues it with a '/': "/admin", "/admin/x/y",
"/%61dmin/x" and "/admin%2Fx" are all under "/admin", but
"/administrator" is not.  The most specific mount that owns the path
handles the request alone, so for "/api/v1/x" a mount of "/api/v1"
wins over a mount of "/api", and no route of a less specific mount,
and no root-level route, can answer it.  When none of the routes of
the owner matches, the owner answers 404 (or 405 when a route of the
owner matches the path but not the method), and the middleware of the
owner runs for that rejection just as it runs for its routes.  A path
under no mount goes to the root router, which holds the routes that
chttpsvr_register_handler and chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler
register directly on the server.

A guard in the middleware of a sub-router therefore covers every path
under its prefix, whatever any other router registers: a root-level
pattern such as "/{a}/{b}/{c}" overlaps every three-segment path, yet
it never answers "/admin/a/b" while "/admin" is mounted.  A mount
nested under another one, such as "/admin/deep" under "/admin", owns
the paths under itself, so the middleware of "/admin" does not run for
them; give the nested mount a guard of its own when it needs one.  Two
sub-routers on the identical prefix share it: the routes of both can
answer, the earlier registration first, and the earlier one answers a
404 or a 405.  Precedence therefore depends only on the prefixes and
patterns that you register, and never on the order of registration.
Within one router, the server tries the routes in registration order;
see chttpsvr_register_handler(3).

The server decodes each raw segment on its own before matching it, so
although "/admin%2Fx" is under "/admin", no route of that mount can
match it, because a route matches whole segments; the mount answers
it with a 404.

The "/" prefix is a special case.  The loop that strips a trailing
slash runs only for a prefix longer than one character, so "/" is kept
as it is, with prefix_len = 1.  A "/" sub-router owns only the exact
request path "/": no other path is under it, and the server routes
every other path, including one that starts with a second slash such
as "//foo", as if the "/" sub-router did not exist.  Every route on a
"/" sub-router is therefore reachable only from the exact path "/".

That makes this prefix a trap for a sub-router that carries its own
middleware: an authenticator or a rate limiter on a "/" sub-router
does not refuse everything outside "/".  A root-level route, or
another sub-router, serves any other path normally, and the middleware
of the "/" sub-router does not run for it.

Use the prefix "/" only when you want to match the exact root path "/"
and nothing else.  To run a middleware for every request, whatever
the path, register it globally with chttpsvr_use; to catch every
request, whatever the path, register routes directly on the server
with chttpsvr_register_handler or chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new chttpsvr_router on success.  NULL when srv is
CHTTPSVR_INVALID, when srv is a stale handle that has already been
destroyed, when prefix is NULL, when prefix does not start with '/',
when prefix contains two consecutive slashes, or when the library
runs out of memory.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_router_on (3),
.BR chttpsvr_router_use (3)
